    "question": {
        "content": "Given the measure of an acute angle in a right triangle, we can tell the ratios of the lengths of the triangle's sides relative to that acute angle.\n\nHere are the approximate ratios for angle measures $55\\degree$, $65\\degree$, and $75\\degree$.\n\nAngle | $55\\degree$ | $65\\degree$ | $75\\degree$\n:-: | :-: | :-: | :-:\n$\\dfrac{\\text{adjacent leg length}}{\\text{hypotenuse length}}$ | $0.57$ | $0.42$ | $0.26$\n$\\dfrac{\\text{opposite leg length}}{\\text{hypotenuse length}}$ | $0.82$ | $0.91$ | $0.97$\n$\\dfrac{\\text{opposite leg length}}{\\text{adjacent leg length}}$ | $1.43$ | $2.14$ | $3.73$\n\n**Use the table to approximate $GH$ in the triangle below.**\n\n\n\n[[☃ image 1]]\n\n[[☃ radio 1]]",
